{"content":[{"c":["RACA*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Derogatory expression used by Jews of the first century ",{"c":["AD"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"}," to show open contempt for another. ",{"c":["Raca"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," is derived from an Aramaic and Hebrew term meaning “empty” or “worthless.” Literally meaning “empty-headed,” ",{"c":["raca"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," probably insinuates an intellectual stupidity or inferiority rather than a moral deficiency. In the OT it is comparable to the worthless lot that Abimelech hired to follow him (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.9.4","r":"Judg.9.4","rawR":"Judg.9.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.9.4","value":"Judg.9.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 9:4"},"), the idle men who gathered around Jephthah (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.11.3","r":"Judg.11.3","rawR":"Judg.11.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.11.3","value":"Judg.11.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"11:3"},"), and the scoundrels who joined up with Jeroboam (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.13.7","r":"2Chr.13.7","rawR":"2Chr.13.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.13.7","value":"2Chr.13.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 13:7"},"). Michal accused David of acting like one of the vulgar fellows [",{"c":["raca"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"] who shamelessly uncovered himself (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.6.20","r":"2Sam.6.20","rawR":"2Sam.6.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.6.20","value":"2Sam.6.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 6:20"},"). Rabbinic literature used this term to describe an immoral, untrained person."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Jesus warned against calling a brother ",{"c":["“Raca!”"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=Matt.5.22","r":"Matt.5.22","rawR":"Matt.5.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.5.22","value":"Matt.5.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mt 5:22"},", ",{"c":["nlt"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"}," mg). According to Jesus, the perpetrator of the insult was to be judged by the highest court of the land and punished by its severest penalty. The commandment against murder (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.20.13","r":"Exod.20.13","rawR":"Exod.20.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.20.13","value":"Exod.20.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 20:13"},") not only prohibited the deed itself but also the thoughts of unrighteous anger and expressions of unwarranted contempt."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Raca","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Jgs 9:4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.9.4","rawQueryValue":"Judg.9.4","sourceRefExact":"Judg.9.4"},{"anchorText":"11:3","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.11.3","rawQueryValue":"Judg.11.3","sourceRefExact":"Judg.11.3"},{"anchorText":"2 Chr 13:7","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Chr.13.7","rawQueryValue":"2Chr.13.7","sourceRefExact":"2Chr.13.7"},{"anchorText":"2 Sm 6:20","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.6.20","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.6.20","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.6.20"},{"anchorText":"Mt 5:22","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Matt.5.22","rawQueryValue":"Matt.5.22","sourceRefExact":"Matt.5.22"},{"anchorText":"Ex 20:13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Exod.20.13","rawQueryValue":"Exod.20.13","sourceRefExact":"Exod.20.13"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"36d9ceccdfe7551323a95ac5cb93b43e35aa24862542804b657bc9d37f83a4dc","sourceItemName":"Raca","sourceItemOrder":12,"sourceMember":"Articles/R.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Raca","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Raca*","title":"Raca*","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}